init
=====

a minimal init for Linux



how to compile
===============

run "make build"
to install, use make install
if you want a install prefix, define DESTDIR as your desired prefix.



what is an init and how to use it?
=============================

init is the first process started by the kernel after boot (PID 1).
It is the parent of all processes on the system and is responsible
for bringing the system up and taking it down cleanly.


SIGNALS
-------

init listens to a few signals to know what to do:

SIGTERM  - go to single-user mode
SIGUSR2  - poweroff
SIGINT   - reboot


MODES
-----

init operates in two main modes:

multi-user  - normal operation, all daemons and services running
single-user - minimal mode, only a root shell on the console


GOING TO SINGLE-USER (su1)
--------------------------

When init receives SIGTERM, it:

1. runs /etc/rc.shutdown to stop services
2. sends SIGTERM to all running processes (graceful stop)
3. waits a moment
4. sends SIGKILL to processes still alive
5. drops into a root shell on the console


SHUTDOWN
--------

When init receives SIGUSR2, it does the same cleanup as above,
but at the end instead of dropping to a shell, it calls the
reboot(2) syscall with the RB_POWEROFF flag, which tells the
kernel to cut power through ACPI.


REBOOT
------

Same as shutdown, but the final syscall uses the RB_AUTOBOOT flag
instead, which makes the kernel restart the machine.


SUMMARY
-------

su1       -> SIGTERM  -> stop services -> kill processes -> root shell
shutdown  -> SIGUSR2  -> stop services -> kill processes -> reboot(RB_POWEROFF)
reboot    -> SIGINT   -> stop services -> kill processes -> reboot(RB_AUTOBOOT)
